<mujoco model="desk_mat">
  <!-- THE WORK SURFACE OF THE `so101-tower` RIG — a STATIC slot, the desk.

       On the rig the arm, the tower and the props all stand on ONE white
       desk, about 65 x 65 cm (measured 2026-09-20). This is that desk: a
       65 x 65 cm white slab whose TOP face is at z = 0.002, carrying the
       site every region hangs off. The tower's foot and the arm's base
       plate stand ON it (they sit at the floor plane z = 0, i.e. 2 mm into
       the slab — static geoms, no contact, and the plate hides the seam);
       the props rest on it at 0.002.

       ⚠ THE SLAB IS OFFSET FROM ITS BODY. The body — and the `surface` site
       the regions are drawn in — stays at world (0.32, 0, 0.001), where the
       first version of this mat put it, so every region rectangle and every
       recorded placement keeps its meaning. The geom is shifted 5.5 cm back
       so the slab spans world x -0.06..0.59, y -0.325..0.325: the arm and
       the tower stand AT THE BACK EDGE of the desk, as on the rig (the arm
       base reaches x -0.056, the tower foot x -0.032..0.071, y -0.16..0.08),
       and the desk extends 59 cm in front of the arm. The earlier 48 x 50
       cm mat started 8 cm IN FRONT of the arm, which is not the rig.

       ⚠ WHY A MAT AND NOT `fixture.xml`. Two reasons the tabletop family's
       2 cm slab does not serve here: (1) the props must rest at the SAME
       height as the arm's base, because that is the geometry the real
       recordings have — a 2 cm platform would move every grasp height;
       (2) the tracer draws it, and the recorded frames show a white desk,
       not a brown slab. `rgba` is the desk's colour for that reason.
       ⚠ 0.81, NOT 0.93: the grey the rig's CAMERAS record the desk at under
       the family's calibrated lights (so101_tower.family, 2026-09-24:
       real median 183 overhead / 155 wrist; albedo = real / light level,
       the two cameras' geometric mean). A whiter desk rendered clipped.

       ⚠⚠ 2 cm THICK, EXTENDING BELOW THE FLOOR PLANE, WITH ITS TOP STILL AT
       z = 0.002. It was a 2 mm slab, and the printed bowl FELL THROUGH IT
       (2026-09-20, in MuJoCo and in ours alike): a box-box contact's normal
       points from the slab's centre toward the other box's centre, and the
       bowl's collision floor is a 4 mm box whose centre sits 2 mm above the
       bowl's base — so once the falling bowl had sunk 3 mm into a soft
       contact the normal FLIPPED, the mat pushed it down onto the ground
       plane, and it rested with its cavity floor coplanar with the mat's
       top, which is what "the bottom of the bowl is white" was. A slab
       thicker than any resting box's half-height cannot flip; 1 cm below
       the floor plane costs nothing (the plane and a static geom never
       contact) and the props still rest at 0.002. The brick never showed
       it: its box centre is 12.5 mm above its base.

       ⚠ `solref`/`solimp` AS ON THE PROPS (`gen_tower_props.CONTACT`): a
       pair's softness is the AVERAGE of its two geoms', so a stiff brick on
       a default desk is only half stiff. -->
  <compiler angle="radian"/>
  <worldbody>
    <body name="mat">
      <geom name="geom" type="box" pos="-0.055 0 -0.009" size="0.325 0.325 0.010" density="800"
            rgba="0.81 0.81 0.79 1" solref="0.01 1" solimp="0.998 0.998 0.001"/>
      <!-- THE SITE THE REGIONS ATTACH TO, on the top face — see
           `fixture.xml` for why it is site-relative and `group="3"`. -->
      <site name="surface" pos="0 0 0.001" size="0.005" group="3"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
